      "source": "e5",
      "predicate": "has symbols not seen there",
      "target": "e9",
      "evidence": "Its symbols are not to be seen in Chichen--the city of the holy and learned men, Itzaes--but are frequently met with in the northern parts of the peninsula"
    },
    {
      "source": "e4",
      "predicate": "has symbols of phallic worship of Nahualts",
      "target": "e7",
      "evidence": "I found the remnants of the phallic worship of the Nahualts"
    },
    {
      "source": "e8",
      "predicate": "worshipped Deity under symbol of",
      "target": "e9",
      "evidence": "CAN and the members of his family worshipped Deity under the symbol of the mastodon's head"
    },
    {
      "source": "e10",
      "predicate": "is ornamented with worship tableau",
      "target": "e9",
      "evidence": "At Chichen a tableau of said worship forms the ornament of the building, designated in the work of Stephens, \"Travels in Yucatan,\" as IGLESIA"
    },
    {
      "source": "e10",
      "predicate": "is north wing of palace and museum",
      "target": "e10",
      "evidence": "being, in fact, the north wing of the palace and museum"
    },
    {
      "source": "e8",
      "predicate": "worshipped sun and fire represented by",
      "target": "e11",
      "evidence": "They also worshipped the sun and fire, which they represented by the same hieroglyph used by the Egyptians for the sun [sun]"
    },
    {
      "source": "e8",
      "predicate": "resembled",
      "target": "e12",
      "evidence": "In this worship of the fire they resembled the Chaldeans and Hindoos"
    },
    {
      "source": "e8",
      "predicate": "differed from",
      "target": "e14",
      "evidence": "but differed from the Egyptians, who had no veneration for this element"
    },
    {
      "source": "e8",
      "predicate": "regarded fire as animal that devoured all things within its reach",
      "target": "e11",
      "evidence": "They regarded it merely as an animal that devoured all things within its reach, and died with all it had swallowed, when replete and satisfied"
    },
    {
      "source": "e8",
      "predicate": "religion replaced by",
      "target": "e15",
      "evidence": "These destroyed Chichen, and established their capital at _Uxmal_. There they erected in all the courts of the palaces, and on the platforms of the temples the symbols of their religion, taking care"
    },
    {
      "source": "e8",
      "predicate": "was replaced by religion of reciprocal forces of nature",
      "target": "e15",
      "evidence": "From certain inscriptions and pictures--in which the _Cans_ are represented crawling on all fours like dogs--sculptured on the facade of their house of worship, it would appear that their religion of the mastodon was replaced by that of the reciprocal forces of nature, imported in the country by the big-nosed invaders, the Nahualts coming from the west"
    },
